WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.599
A Fenwick tree, or Binary Indexed Tree, indexes prefix blocks using the lowest set bit of each one-based index.

00:00:08.599 --> 00:00:15.184
The expression i bitwise-and negative i isolates the largest power of two dividing i.

00:00:15.184 --> 00:00:19.600
Each slot stores the sum of a block of that exact length.

00:00:19.875 --> 00:00:27.875
To compute a prefix sum up to index i, add the current slot and strip its lowest set bit repeatedly until reaching zero.

00:00:27.875 --> 00:00:34.008
Because an integer has at most log N set bits, the loop finishes in a handful of iterations.

00:00:34.008 --> 00:00:37.475
Range sums follow by subtracting two prefix queries.

00:00:37.750 --> 00:00:46.935
When element i changes by delta, add the lowest set bit to i at each step to visit every block covering that position.

00:00:46.935 --> 00:00:53.084
A flat array and simple bit arithmetic keep the constant factor tiny in Python.

00:00:53.084 --> 00:00:57.910
Point updates and prefix queries both run in logarithmic time.

00:00:58.167 --> 00:01:04.927
Fenwick trees excel at counting inversions or dynamic order statistics during a left-to-right sweep.

00:01:04.927 --> 00:01:12.499
Query how many previously seen values exceed the current element, then insert the current element into the tree.

00:01:12.499 --> 00:01:16.218
Each step takes logarithmic time over compressed ranks.
